    {
      "turn": 1,
      "doctor_question": "Hello, I’m glad you came in. What brings you in today?",
      "targeted_symptom": "S002",
      "patient_response": "Hi, yes, thank you for seeing me. I'm just... I'm at my wit's end, to be completely honest with you. What brings me in is that I just can't seem to keep my mind on a task long enough to finish it anymore. And it's not just the mundane stuff, it's happening even when it's incredibly important. \n\nI'm a computer support specialist, so a lot of my job is highly technical, digging into systems when things go wrong. Just the other day, for example, I had this major client whose whole system was bottlenecking, so I sat down to trace the network problem. I pulled up the server logs, which is standard procedure, you know, looking for the dropped packets or the error codes. But I'll be sitting there, staring at the screen, and ten minutes later I'll suddenly realize that I've just reread the exact same block of log entries three times. Three times! And I still don't know what they actually say. \n\nIt's like my eyes are scanning the text, the IP addresses, the timestamps, but absolutely nothing is registering in my brain. And then I have to start all over again, which is incredibly frustrating when you have an entire office waiting on you to fix their connection so they can get back to work. It's just... it's like my brain just unplugs itself right in the middle of a thought, and I don't even notice until I've wasted ten minutes staring at a monitor. It's really starting to scare me, because I have a graduate degree, I know how to do this work, I've been doing it for years, but now I just sit there spinning my wheels.",
